WEBVTT

00:00.820 --> 00:05.880
I have a function here defined
as x squared minus 5x plus 6.

00:05.880 --> 00:07.600
And what I want
us to think about

00:07.600 --> 00:12.060
is what other forms we can
write this function in if we,

00:12.060 --> 00:14.719
say, wanted to find the
0s of this function.

00:14.719 --> 00:17.260
If we wanted to figure out where
does this function intersect

00:17.260 --> 00:20.370
the x-axis, what form
would we put this in?

00:20.370 --> 00:23.110
And then another form
for maybe finding out

00:23.110 --> 00:24.800
what's the minimum
value of this.

00:24.800 --> 00:27.780
We see that we have a positive
coefficient on the x squared

00:27.780 --> 00:28.280
term.

00:28.280 --> 00:31.229
This is going to be an
upward-opening parabola.

00:31.229 --> 00:32.770
But what's the
minimum point of this?

00:32.770 --> 00:35.364
Or even better, what's the
vertex of this parabola

00:35.364 --> 00:36.030
right over here?

00:36.030 --> 00:38.190
So if the function looks
something like this,

00:38.190 --> 00:41.180
we could use one
form of the function

00:41.180 --> 00:44.160
to figure out where does
it intersect the x-axis.

00:44.160 --> 00:46.277
So where does it
intersect the x-axis?

00:46.277 --> 00:48.360
And maybe we can manipulate
it to get another form

00:48.360 --> 00:49.985
to figure out what's
the minimum point.

00:49.985 --> 00:52.750
What's this point right
over here for this function?

00:52.750 --> 00:54.917
I don't even know if the
function looks like this.

00:54.917 --> 00:56.500
So I encourage you
to pause this video

00:56.500 --> 01:00.450
and try to manipulate this
into those two different forms.

01:00.450 --> 01:01.590
So let's work on it.

01:01.590 --> 01:03.590
So in order to find the
roots, the easiest thing

01:03.590 --> 01:06.020
I can think of doing
is trying to factor

01:06.020 --> 01:08.770
this quadratic
expression which is

01:08.770 --> 01:10.341
being used to define
this function.

01:10.341 --> 01:11.840
So we could think
about, well, let's

01:11.840 --> 01:17.170
think of two numbers whose
product is positive 6

01:17.170 --> 01:22.240
and whose sum is negative 5.

01:22.240 --> 01:25.090
So since their
product is positive,

01:25.090 --> 01:26.977
we know that they
have the same sign.

01:26.977 --> 01:29.560
And if they have the same sign
but we get to a negative value,

01:29.560 --> 01:32.280
that means they both
must be negative.

01:32.280 --> 01:35.800
So let's see-- negative 2
times negative 3 is positive 6.

01:35.800 --> 01:39.890
Negative 2 plus negative
3 is negative 5.

01:39.890 --> 01:41.847
So we could rewrite f of x.

01:41.847 --> 01:43.180
And so let me write it this way.

01:43.180 --> 01:47.440
We could write f of
x as being equal to x

01:47.440 --> 01:51.580
minus 2 times x minus 3.

01:51.580 --> 01:54.160
Now, how does this help
us find the zeroes?

01:54.160 --> 01:58.295
Well, in what situations is
this right-hand expression,

01:58.295 --> 02:00.861
is this expression on the right
hand going to be equal to 0?

02:00.861 --> 02:02.860
Well, it's the product
of these two expressions.

02:02.860 --> 02:06.786
If either one of these is equal
to 0, 0 times anything is 0.

02:06.786 --> 02:08.570
0 times anything else is 0.

02:08.570 --> 02:13.240
So this whole thing is going to
be 0 if x minus 2 is equal to 0

02:13.240 --> 02:17.160
or x minus 3 is equal to 0.

02:17.160 --> 02:19.110
Add 2 to both sides
of this equation.

02:19.110 --> 02:26.360
You get x is equal to
2 or x is equal to 3.

02:26.360 --> 02:31.190
So those are the two
zeroes for this function,

02:31.190 --> 02:32.240
I guess you could say.

02:32.240 --> 02:33.698
And we could already
think about it

02:33.698 --> 02:35.300
a little bit in
terms of graphing it.

02:37.980 --> 02:41.180
So let's try to
graph this thing.

02:41.180 --> 02:43.020
So this is x equals 1.

02:43.020 --> 02:44.480
This is x equals 2.

02:44.480 --> 02:47.860
This is x equals 3
right over there.

02:47.860 --> 02:49.840
So that's our x-axis.

02:49.840 --> 02:53.890
That, you could say, is our
y is equal to f of x axis.

02:53.890 --> 02:59.960
And we're seeing that we
intersect both here and here.

02:59.960 --> 03:03.590
When x is equal to 2,
this f of x is equal to 0.

03:03.590 --> 03:06.742
When x is equal to 3,
f of x is equal to 0.

03:06.742 --> 03:08.700
And you could substitute
either of these values

03:08.700 --> 03:09.730
into the original expression.

03:09.730 --> 03:11.229
And you'll see it's
going to get you

03:11.229 --> 03:13.390
to 0 because that is
the same thing as that.

03:13.390 --> 03:14.610
Now, what about the vertex?

03:14.610 --> 03:16.840
What form could we write
this original thing

03:16.840 --> 03:19.069
in order to pick out the vertex?

03:19.069 --> 03:20.610
Well, we're already
a little familiar

03:20.610 --> 03:21.735
with completing the square.

03:21.735 --> 03:27.520
And when you complete the
square with this expression,

03:27.520 --> 03:30.470
that seems to be a pretty good
way of thinking about what

03:30.470 --> 03:32.530
the minimum value
of this function is.

03:32.530 --> 03:34.690
So let's just do
that right over here.

03:34.690 --> 03:36.280
So I'm just going to rewrite it.

03:36.280 --> 03:43.759
So we get f of x is equal
to x squared minus 5x.

03:43.759 --> 03:46.050
And I'm just going to throw
the plus 6 right over here.

03:46.050 --> 03:47.990
And I'm giving myself
some real estate

03:47.990 --> 03:50.750
because what I need to do, what
I want to think about doing,

03:50.750 --> 03:53.504
is adding and subtracting
the same value.

03:53.504 --> 03:56.170
So I'm going to add it here, and
I'm going to subtract it there.

03:56.170 --> 03:58.530
And I can do that because
then I've just added 0.

03:58.530 --> 04:01.100
I haven't changed the value
of this right-hand side.

04:01.100 --> 04:04.660
But I want to do that so that
this part that I've underlined

04:04.660 --> 04:07.280
in this magenta color, so that
this part right over here,

04:07.280 --> 04:08.720
is a perfect square.

04:08.720 --> 04:10.160
And we've done
this multiple times

04:10.160 --> 04:11.270
when we've completed the square.

04:11.270 --> 04:12.730
I encourage you to
watch those videos

04:12.730 --> 04:14.490
if you need a little
bit of a review on it.

04:14.490 --> 04:16.050
But the general idea
is this is going

04:16.050 --> 04:19.230
to be a perfect
square if we take

04:19.230 --> 04:21.410
this coefficient
right over here.

04:21.410 --> 04:23.170
We take negative 5.

04:23.170 --> 04:28.220
We take 1/2 of that, which is
negative 5/2, and we square it.

04:28.220 --> 04:31.840
So we could write this
as plus negative--

04:31.840 --> 04:34.090
what's negative 5/2 squared?

04:34.090 --> 04:37.740
So I could write this--
negative 5/2 squared.

04:37.740 --> 04:39.320
Well, if we square
a negative number,

04:39.320 --> 04:40.695
it's just going
to be a positive.

04:40.695 --> 04:42.800
So it's going to be the
same thing as 5/2 squared.

04:42.800 --> 04:44.510
5 squared is 25.

04:44.510 --> 04:45.940
2 squared is 4.

04:45.940 --> 04:47.470
So this is going
to be plus 25/4.

04:51.520 --> 04:54.410
Now, once again, if we want
this equality to be true,

04:54.410 --> 04:56.830
we either have to add the
same thing to both sides.

04:56.830 --> 04:58.531
Or if we're just
operating on one side,

04:58.531 --> 05:00.280
if we added it to that
side, we could just

05:00.280 --> 05:01.820
subtract it from that side.

05:01.820 --> 05:04.950
And we haven't changed the
total value on that side.

05:04.950 --> 05:07.850
So we added 25/4, and
we subtracted 25/4.

05:10.460 --> 05:12.492
So what is this part
right over here?

05:12.492 --> 05:14.200
What does this become,
the part that I've

05:14.200 --> 05:15.117
underlined in magenta?

05:15.117 --> 05:17.116
Well, this is going to
be-- the whole reason why

05:17.116 --> 05:18.950
we engineered it in
this way is so that this

05:18.950 --> 05:25.140
could be x minus 5/2 squared.

05:25.140 --> 05:26.680
And I encourage
you to verify this.

05:26.680 --> 05:29.140
And we go into more
detail about why

05:29.140 --> 05:32.209
taking 1/2 the coefficient here
and then squaring it, adding it

05:32.209 --> 05:34.250
there and then subtracting
there, why that works.

05:34.250 --> 05:36.740
We do that in the completing
the square videos.

05:36.740 --> 05:38.369
But these two things,
you can verify

05:38.369 --> 05:39.410
that they are equivalent.

05:39.410 --> 05:41.050
So that's that part.

05:41.050 --> 05:43.160
And now we just have to
simplify 6 minus 25/4.

05:46.290 --> 05:50.210
So 6 could be rewritten as 24/4.

05:50.210 --> 05:58.670
24/4 minus 25/4 is negative 1/4,
so minus 1/4, just like that.

05:58.670 --> 06:01.960
So we've rewritten
our original function

06:01.960 --> 06:08.600
as f of x is equal to x
minus 5/2 squared minus 1/4.

06:08.600 --> 06:12.400
Now, why is this
form interesting?

06:12.400 --> 06:15.180
Well, one way to think
about it is this part

06:15.180 --> 06:17.790
is always going to
be non-negative.

06:17.790 --> 06:21.401
The minimum value of this part
in magenta is going to be 0.

06:21.401 --> 06:21.900
Why?

06:21.900 --> 06:23.700
Because we're
squaring this thing.

06:23.700 --> 06:25.310
If you're taking
something like this--

06:25.310 --> 06:27.060
and we're just dealing
with real numbers--

06:27.060 --> 06:29.000
and you're squaring
it, you're not

06:29.000 --> 06:31.090
going to be able to
get a negative value.

06:31.090 --> 06:35.460
At the minimum value,
this is going to be 0.

06:35.460 --> 06:37.962
And then it obviously could
be positive values, as well.

06:37.962 --> 06:40.170
So if we want to think about
when does this thing hit

06:40.170 --> 06:43.010
its minimum value-- well,
it hits its minimum value

06:43.010 --> 06:44.770
when you're squaring 0.

06:44.770 --> 06:46.240
And when are you squaring 0?

06:46.240 --> 06:51.510
Well, you're squaring 0 when
x minus 5/2 is equal to 0,

06:51.510 --> 06:54.660
or when x is equal
to 5/2 if you just

06:54.660 --> 06:58.250
want to add 5/2 to both
sides of that equation.

06:58.250 --> 07:01.970
So this thing hits its minimum
value when x is equal to 5/2.

07:01.970 --> 07:06.710
And then what is y, or what is
f of x, when x is equal to 5/2?

07:06.710 --> 07:10.920
f of 5/2-- and once again, you
could use any of those forms

07:10.920 --> 07:12.190
to evaluate 5/2.

07:12.190 --> 07:14.310
But it's really
easy in this form.

07:14.310 --> 07:17.710
When x is equal to 5/2, this
term right over here becomes 0.

07:17.710 --> 07:19.350
0 squared, 0.

07:19.350 --> 07:21.070
You're just left
with negative 1/4.

07:25.180 --> 07:27.170
So another way to think
about it is our vertex

07:27.170 --> 07:35.500
is at the point x equals
5/2, y equals negative 1/4.

07:35.500 --> 07:36.900
So x equals 5/2.

07:36.900 --> 07:38.970
That's the same
thing as 2 and 1/2.

07:38.970 --> 07:41.710
So x equals 5/2.

07:41.710 --> 07:44.500
And y is equal to negative 1/4.

07:44.500 --> 07:48.830
So if that is negative 1, 1/4
would be something like that.

07:48.830 --> 07:50.700
So that right over
there is the vertex.

07:50.700 --> 07:52.450
That is the point--
let me make it clear--

07:52.450 --> 07:57.830
that's the point 5/2
comma negative 1/4.

07:57.830 --> 07:59.804
And what's cool is we've
just used this form

07:59.804 --> 08:01.720
to figure out the minimum
point, to figure out

08:01.720 --> 08:03.550
the vertex in this case.

08:03.550 --> 08:07.350
And then we can use the
roots as two other points

08:07.350 --> 08:14.570
to get a rough sketch of what
this parabola will actually

08:14.570 --> 08:15.375
look like.

08:15.375 --> 08:17.875
So the interesting-- or I guess
the takeaway from this video

08:17.875 --> 08:21.310
is just to realize that we can
rewrite this in different forms

08:21.310 --> 08:26.000
depending on what we're
trying to understand

08:26.000 --> 08:28.550
about this function.
